<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">HMS Beagle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1820">1820</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">HMS Beagle was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> brig-sloop launched in 1820 and converted for survey work. On her second voyage, from 1831 to 1836 under Captain Robert FitzRoy, she surveyed the coasts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> and sailed round the world, carrying the young naturalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-darwin">Charles Darwin</a>, whose observations, including those in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galapagos-islands">Galápagos Islands</a>, shaped his theory of evolution.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hms-beagle.jpg" alt="HMS Beagle" width="320" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: R. T. Pritchett · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PSM_V57_D097_Hms_beagle_in_the_straits_of_magellan.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
